# MTAB(5)

## NAME

mtab - mounted file system table

## DESCRIPTION

*Mtab* resides in directory */etc* and contains a table of devices mounted by the *mount*
command. *Umount* removes entries, and *df (1M)* reads the table to find out what to report on
and where each file system is mounted.

**Note: the table is a text file here, not v7's binary record.** Each entry is one line, and
the fields are separated by blanks: the name of the block special file, as *mount* was given
it; the directory it was mounted on; and, when the file system was mounted read-only, the word
**ro**. Trailing slashes are stripped from both names and nothing else is; in particular the
special file keeps its directories, where v7 threw away everything through the last `/`.

```
/dev/md1 /mnt
/dev/md2 /usr/src ro
```

v7's entry is 64 bytes - two null-padded 32-byte name fields, written by copying the program's
own **struct mtab** array straight to the file. That arithmetic does not survive this machine.
A **char[32]** occupies 32/6 rounded up = 6 words = 36 char-units and the next member of a
structure starts on a word boundary, so **sizeof(struct mtab)** is 72 rather than 64: v7's own
code writes 64-byte records out of 72-byte objects, every entry after the first is read back
eight bytes out of step, and *mount* with no argument prints rubbish. See *cmd/mount/mtab.c*,
which is the one implementation of this format and is compiled into all three programs that
read or write it.

The number of entries is bounded by **NMOUNT** in <*sys/param.h*>, which is the number of file
systems the kernel can have mounted at once. It is 8 on this system and one of the eight is the
root, which never appears here, so this file holds at most seven.

No file is shipped on the root image. A missing */etc/mtab* reads as an empty table, and the
first *mount* of a boot creates it.

This table is present only so people can look at it. It does not matter to *mount* if there are
duplicated entries nor to *umount* if a name cannot be found - though this *umount* does say
so, on the standard error, having just unmounted the file system anyway.

## FILES

/etc/mtab

## SEE ALSO

mount(1M), umount(1M), df(1M)
